                <pb facs="0133" n="31" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg31"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72119/8543b: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Wallace</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-12-30T16:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 30,
                                1919, 4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>9465. Please inform the Supreme Council that in cases where decisions,
                    resolutions, or actions are taken by that body which do not particularly
                    interest the United States and are agreed to without <pb facs="0134" n="32"
                        xml:id="pg32"/> your specific approval, they should be so worded as to
                    clearly indicate that they represent the views of only those powers which have
                    agreed to them. In connection with this it is suggested that it should be made
                    clear that the expression “Principal Allied and Associated Powers” should only
                    be used when you have agreed to its use in any particular instance.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
